+## Key and password files
+
+One rule reads every key or password file: the HubSpot key (`DESK_HUBSPOT_KEY_FILE`), the live-model key (`DESK_LIVE_KEY_FILE`), the SMTP password (`DESK_SMTPP_PASSWORD_FILE`), the mailbox password (`REAL_IMAP_n_PASSWORD_FILE`), the n8n API key of `scripts/n8n_api.py` (`N8N_API_KEY_FILE`) and the key of the demo model server. It lives in one function (`read_secret_file` in `src/lead_desk/envnum.py`); nothing else in the code opens such a file. Every refusal text names the setting and never shows the path or the key.
+
+* What the file holds: one line of at least 8 printable ASCII characters. The file may be at most 4096 bytes, counting the line break, and is kept as text (UTF-8). Only the line break at the very end of the file is removed; a blank at the start, in the middle or at the end (a plain space, a tab, a no-break space) is refused. The one exception is the SMTP password, whose plain spaces the desk drops wherever they stand (a tab, a no-break space or any other blank is still refused). The mailbox password file follows the SMTP password rule: spaces inside are dropped. A hidden first character that some editors add (a byte-order mark) is accepted and removed. Three faults are told apart, each with its own fixed text: empty or too short, a character that is not printable ASCII, and a space inside a key. A file of several lines, or one that is not UTF-8 text, is refused too.
+* What kind of file: a regular file. A folder, a named pipe (a file that other programs write into), a device (a name for a piece of hardware) or a symbolic link (a name that points to another file) is refused. A folder or a link is refused at once; the pipe and the device are opened only to be refused (no bytes are read from them). A link in a folder above the file is followed; the file itself must be the real file, because a link can be pointed at another file after you checked it. The desk opens the file without following a link and then looks again at the name: a link, or a name that now points at another file, is refused ("the file changed while it was being opened: try again").
+* Who may use it (Linux, macOS and other POSIX systems, which keep permission bits): a file that other users can read, write or run is refused with "the file can be used by other users: copy it to a normal file, run chmod 600 on it, and name the copy where you named this file". The message never prints the key or the path. `chmod 600` (the command that makes a file readable and writable by its owner only) makes the file private; so does starting the shell with `umask 077` (a setting that makes every new file private) before you create the file. There is no way to switch the refusal off, and only the permission bits of the file are checked: who owns the file is not checked, and neither are access lists (extra permissions that some systems attach to a file apart from its mode), so a file that passes can still be open to someone through an access list or because the person who reads it is the system administrator. Windows has no matching mode bits, so the permission bits are not checked there; the checks on the kind of file and on a link still apply (not tried on Windows by the author: the link tests run only on POSIX systems); keep the file in a folder only you can open. Between the check of the name and the open of the file on Windows, a name could be pointed at another file; the POSIX systems refuse a link in the open call itself. Mounts this rule refuses, as far as is known: Docker file secrets and Kubernetes secret volumes often show the mode 0444 or 0644, and Kubernetes secret volumes also give each key as a link, so such a file is refused. Filesystems that report the mode 0777 for every file (a Windows drive seen from WSL, FAT, some bind mounts) are refused too. For all of these, copy the file to a regular file on a normal disk and run chmod 600 on it. Docker and Kubernetes were not tried by the author: this is what is reported about them, not something tested here.
+* When the file is read: the HubSpot key file and the SMTP password file: each is read at start and again before every call (a bad file at start stops the start, exit code 4). The live-model key file is read at start and again at its first call, and the key is kept in memory after that (a file replaced before the first call is the one used). The mailbox password file is not read at start; it is read each time the mailbox is counted. Nothing is kept from the start-up read of the HubSpot and SMTP files, so each of these files must stay in place and readable for as long as the desk runs.
+* Whether the key is valid at the vendor cannot be checked without a network call.
 
